WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.439 --> 00:00:02.510
- [Reporter] This is a spike protein.

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.760
It's what gives the coronavirus
its unmistakable look

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.750
and scientists think it may
be the key to why new variants

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:11.480
of the virus are becoming
more transmissible.

00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:13.314
- A new study by the
CDC is raising the alarm

00:00:13.314 --> 00:00:16.090
on the coronavirus variant
spreading across the country.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:18.370
- The fast-spreading COVID-19 variant

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:21.040
first found in the UK
is now evolving in a way

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.930
that could make existing
vaccines less effective.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.300
- [Reporter] New research shows

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.480
that the more transmissible
variant from the UK,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:33.490
called B117, will likely
become the most common version

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.490
of the virus in the US.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.380
- The big concern is that, with
more transmissible variant,

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.570
you have more cases and
you have more chance

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:43.450
for superspreading events
and epidemic surges

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:45.790
which overwhelm our healthcare systems.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:46.623
- [Reporter] Here's the science

00:00:46.623 --> 00:00:49.500
behind why these new
variants are spreading faster

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:51.403
and what this could mean for vaccines.

00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:53.150
(soft music)

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.340
Like other viruses, when
the coronavirus replicates,

00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.190
small genetic mistakes,
or mutations, can occur.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.930
- You can be almost certain

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.410
that as long as there's a lot of virus

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:08.220
circulating in the community,
there will be the evolution

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.620
of mutants because that's what viruses do.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:12.968
- Many of those do nothing.

00:01:12.968 --> 00:01:17.050
Some of those actually
confer a survival benefit

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:20.847
for the virus to transmit more
effectively, for instance.

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:23.590
- [Reporter] Abraar Karan
is a global health physician

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.490
who has been studying
the coronavirus pandemic.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:27.830
- And so, as it does that,

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.690
it has a better chance
of spreading to new hosts

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.020
and continuing to
replicate in those hosts.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.470
- [Reporter] Karan says this
is likely what is happening

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.560
with the new, more transmissible variants

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:37.992
appearing around the world.

00:01:37.992 --> 00:01:40.500
Recent research has shown that a number

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.510
of these new versions of
the virus have mutations

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:45.464
that affect the spike protein.

00:01:45.464 --> 00:01:48.450
Take a look at this
model of the coronavirus.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.730
You can see each spike protein

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:54.400
is made up of three identical
parts known as protomers.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.000
These protomers have the
ability to change their position

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.030
from closed to open, affecting how easily

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:03.810
they can bind to and infect human cells.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.350
- You can visualize it like a flower.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.020
So you've got petals on the outside,

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.590
the really obvious thing that you see.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:10.660
There are three of them.

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:13.050
The three of them have
to bind to the receptors.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:15.010
- [Reporter] Jeremy Luban is a virologist

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:16.580
who studies the coronavirus.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.150
He explains that when
the protomers are down,

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.900
infection is more difficult.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.860
But when these parts are up
and open, it's much easier.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.210
In the first version of the coronavirus

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:29.880
that originated in China in 2019,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.070
these protomers usually
had a closed shape,

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.450
which may have made it less transmissible

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:37.691
than some more recent
versions of the virus.

00:02:37.691 --> 00:02:40.310
This is another view of a spike protein

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:43.330
that helps to show where
these mutations occur.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.330
In the winter of 2020,

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:49.530
one mutation, known as D614G, emerged.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.750
It appears in this region
of the spike protein.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:55.350
This mutation made it
more likely for the spike

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.460
to have an open shape,
increasing the virus' ability

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.240
to infect human cells.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:02.181
Soon after the mutation emerged,

00:03:02.181 --> 00:03:05.040
variants that had it took over.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.200
- So by June, pretty much all
the viruses around the planet

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.420
had this change, this mutation.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:15.770
Our belief is that it
supplanted the original virus

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.070
because it was more transmissible.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.840
- [Reporter] Luban thinks
this is what is happening

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.070
with some of the new variants

00:03:22.070 --> 00:03:24.570
that are emerging around
the world as well.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.050
- In the United Kingdom,

00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.890
that variant really expanded very rapidly.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.540
It was first seen in September

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.590
and now it's the dominant
version of the virus

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.740
circulating in the United Kingdom.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:38.810
Does indeed appear as if
the same thing happened

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:40.200
in South Africa.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.090
- [Reporter] Another element

00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:44.090
that affects the spike
protein is antibodies.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.270
These are proteins that defend the body

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:49.240
against the coronavirus
by blocking the ability

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.413
of the spike protein to attach to cells,

00:03:51.413 --> 00:03:54.100
which prevents the virus from infecting.

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.550
Antibodies are produced
by the immune system

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.320
in response to infection or vaccines,

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:02.140
and so far, the new coronavirus variants

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.940
scientists are worried
about all have mutations

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.010
that affect the spike protein.

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:09.920
Here are some that
scientists are focusing on.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.920
The first is called N501Y,
which was first detected

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:16.640
in the variants from
the UK and South Africa

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.840
in the second half of 2020.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.570
This mutation is located in
a region of the spike protein

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:26.790
known as the receptor
binding domain, or the RBD.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.420
This is the main location

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.800
where the virus attaches to
human cells and infects them.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:36.740
The N501Y mutation seems to
be helping the virus bind,

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.770
which could help explain
the higher transmissibility.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:40.898
The second mutation

00:04:40.898 --> 00:04:44.203
that scientists are
focusing on is called E484K.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:46.810
It's found in variants

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:50.020
from South Africa and Brazil
and reduces the ability

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.020
of certain antibodies to bind to the RBD,

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:56.020
which helps the virus'
chances of infecting cells.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:04:58.830
- What the antibody
recognizes is the shape.

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.928
If you're playing with
blocks and you have triangles

00:05:01.928 --> 00:05:05.480
and circles and you have to fit them

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:08.760
into the appropriate holes,

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.083
you can't fit a circle
into a triangular hole.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.810
It's analogous to that.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.540
If the virus then changes

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.550
so that it's no longer a triangular hole

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:21.170
but it's a circular hole,

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:23.800
then the antibody will no longer fit.

00:05:23.800 --> 00:05:25.490
- [Reporter] Scientists have also found

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:29.100
that some of the new coronavirus
variants have parts missing

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:32.230
in a region that comes off
the side of the spike protein.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.980
This is known as the N-terminal domain.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:37.060
- Some of those mutations

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:41.190
are removing surfaces of the protein

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:43.610
that other antibodies bind to.

00:05:43.610 --> 00:05:47.900
If the virus mutates that
surface or deletes it,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.540
those antibodies are
no longer going to work

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:51.890
to block the virus.

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:54.110
- [Reporter] Scientists
say this may help explain

00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:57.570
why current vaccines may
be becoming less effective

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.270
against some new variants.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.860
This is why experts are urging caution.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.867
- Variants are gonna continue emerging

00:06:03.867 --> 00:06:07.190
and we need a public health
strategy that addresses it.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:09.270
We have to focus on
getting better ventilation.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.210
We have to get people
better personal protection;

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:13.960
better, higher-grade masks
that can filter better;

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.640
we have to test; we have to trace;

00:06:15.640 --> 00:06:18.320
we have to isolate the
fundamentals of epidemic control.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:20.440
- [Reporter] And Karan
says that while vaccines

00:06:20.440 --> 00:06:23.740
should still be effective in
preventing severe illness,

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.826
a booster variant shot may
be needed later in the year.

00:06:26.826 --> 00:06:29.130
- They work extremely well right now,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:33.817
right, With Moderna and
Pfizer 95% vaccine efficacy.

00:06:33.817 --> 00:06:35.960
They will start to work less well

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:38.260
against some variants into the future,

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:40.770
but that is to be expected

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:44.240
and that is why vaccines
will be a major part

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.197
of COVID control for
the foreseeable future.

00:06:47.197 --> 00:06:49.350
- [Reporter] Experts continue to emphasize

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:51.550
the importance of getting vaccinated

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:54.720
along with other strategies
to keep transmission low

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:55.928
and stop the spread.

00:06:55.928 --> 00:06:58.345
(soft music)

